<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>KhaozEngine.Gpu.Metal</PackageId>
    <!-- Shared engine version line: <KhaozEngineVersion> in Directory.Build.props. -->
    <Version>$(KhaozEngineVersion)</Version>
    <!-- The framework is net10.0, NOT net10.0-macos, and that is decision M-P1 rather than an oversight. A
         macOS-only TFM would stop this assembly compiling on the Linux ci.yml leg and both Windows legs, and
         would stop KhaozEngine.Render.Tests referencing it unconditionally, which is where its device-free tests
         live. The macOS boundary is carried instead by [SupportedOSPlatformGuard("macos")] over
         [MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)] [SupportedOSPlatform("macos")] bodies, which is P1's
         apparatus rather than V-P1's: Vulkan is not an OS-specific API and Metal is, so the Direct3D 11 pattern
         applies here verbatim while the Vulkan package's deliberate absence of it does not. With warnings as
         errors, CA1416 turns that boundary into a compiler-enforced rule rather than a convention. The pattern
         is already proven twice in this repository: D3D11ThreadingProbe on the Windows side, and
         MetalFrameCapture, which lives in KhaozEngine.Gpu today with objc_msgSend declarations and loads
         harmlessly on Linux and Windows because nothing calls it there. -->
    <!-- Unsafe, and by construction rather than by choice (M-P2). The Objective-C interop is [LibraryImport] over
         objc_msgSend with blittable-only signatures, and two things on that path have no safe spelling: an
         Objective-C BLOCK is a struct whose third field is a raw function pointer (which is what carries the
         [UnmanagedCallersOnly] completion handler M-F3 specifies), and the array setters take pointers to
         caller-owned handle and offset arrays. Same flag KhaozEngine.Gpu, Render2D, Render3D, Gpu.D3D11 and
         Gpu.Vulkan already carry. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Description>Engine-owned native Metal backend for the KhaozEngine.Gpu seam, the DEFAULT backend on macOS since 17.40.0 (carried by the KhaozEngine.Game2D and KhaozEngine.Game3D umbrellas since 18.0.0, along with its two siblings, because KhaozEngine.Gpu builds no device of its own any more), and phase 4 of the staged native GPU backend program. IT CREATES A DEVICE, WITH A TIMELINE AND REAL RESOURCES, THAT RECORDS AND SUBMITS: KhaozEngineMetal.Register(), the functional machine-capability probe and HEADLESS device creation are real (the engine-owned Objective-C interop layer, an MTLDevice and one MTLCommandQueue, KE_METAL_DEVICE selection, KE_METAL_VALIDATION reporting, a command-buffer error latch that closes #427 for this leg, and a drain before teardown), the three phase-4 verification spikes ran on real hardware, that device CREATES RESOURCES (buffers, textures, samplers and fences through IGpuDevice.Factory, the shared WRAP sampler pair, the device-level uploads on a setup command buffer instead of a queue submit per call, and Map with a read drain the incumbent did not have), and it owns an MTLSharedEvent completion timeline plus a command list that takes a fresh MTLCommandBuffer per Begin, keeps one encoder open at a time and submits under one lock behind a setup flush. It COMPILES SHADERS: GLSL to SPIR-V to MSL under two pinned option sets, one MTLLibrary and MTLFunction per stage, and a per-program binding table read out of the emitted MSL through each stage's own SPIR-V decorations rather than counted on the CPU, which is the defect class three production incidents in this engine came from. It BINDS RENDER TARGETS: framebuffers that create no native object at all, a render pass deferred to the first draw so a clear folds into that attachment's loadAction, explicit store actions, the clear-only pass reproduced at both forcing sites, and the viewport and scissor emitted on a framebuffer change only with the scissor gated on the seam's own ScissorTestEnabled. A clear lands on the attachment the caller named, where the Veldrid Metal backend collapsed every clear onto attachment 0 and therefore never cleared a multi-target framebuffer's other attachments, and the KE_METAL_CLEAR switch that reproduced that for the golden A/B was removed at rollout gate 1. It REPORTS ON ITSELF: the whole GpuCapabilities set, held at zero permitted differences from the Veldrid Metal backend until that backend was deleted in 18.0.0, every GpuDeviceCounters channel a device with no swapchain has, a softwareAdapter answer of false with confidence rather than null, and a Metal GPU frame capture that takes this backend's own command-queue pointer instead of reflecting into Veldrid's private field. IT PRESENTS: windowed creation builds a real CAMetalLayer over the Cocoa NSWindow, the drawable is acquired at the present boundary and counted into the acquire-wait pair the incumbent throttled on with nothing watching, displaySyncEnabled is written unconditionally where the incumbent wrote it inside three values of an enum deprecated since macOS 10.15, a nil drawable binds a device-owned orphan target so the frame still records, submits and counts with only its present skipped, and a resize is queued and applied at the boundary after a drain. Neither IGpuDevice nor IGpuCommandList has an unbuilt member left. Its CI leg is metal-native on hosted macos-26, running the whole suite on every trigger against the committed metal-native golden family it has owned since 17.41.0, under KE_METAL_REQUIRED=1 and Metal's API-validation layer. Targets net10.0 (not net10.0-macos) with [SupportedOSPlatformGuard("macos")] entry points over NoInlining bodies, so the assembly compiles and its device-free tests run on Linux and Windows while the Objective-C interop stays off the load path there. Carries NO third-party package at all, which is a first for this program: there is no maintained managed Metal binding to take, so the interop is an engine-owned [LibraryImport] layer over objc_msgSend. Carries no Veldrid edge of its own either, asserted on both the project graph and the built IL. Spec: docs/design/METAL-NATIVE-BACKEND-DESIGN-2026-08-09.md.</Description>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../KhaozEngine.Gpu/KhaozEngine.Gpu.csproj" />
    <!-- The engine logger, for the lines the interop spike writes when it records what a real device answered.
         Declared explicitly rather than ridden transitively through KhaozEngine.Gpu, the same way Gpu.D3D11 and
         Gpu.Vulkan declare it: a backend that logs should say so in its own project file. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../KhaozEngine.Diagnostics/KhaozEngine.Diagnostics.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- NO PackageReference, deliberately, and this is decision M-P3. Phase 2 took Vortice.Direct3D11 and phase 3
         took Silk.NET.Vulkan on the reasoning that owning the BACKEND and owning the BINDING are different
         things. That reasoning is unchanged and it has nothing to point at here: Silk.NET ships no Metal,
         Vortice ships no Metal, and Apple ships no managed binding of any kind. Vendoring
         Veldrid.MetalBindings is rejected by name (section 3.1), so the interop is hand-rolled and this package
         is the only backend in the program whose ArchitectureTests.ThirdPartyHomes row is empty. Do not add one
         for symmetry. -->
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="KhaozEngine.Render.Tests" />
    <!-- The uniform ring's SEMANTIC tests are shared with both other native backends (M-P5, M-T5): one internal
         test-only interface and all THREE adapters live in KhaozEngine.TestSupport.Gpu, which is
         IsPackable=false and ships nothing, so 2.8's no-shared-PRODUCTION-home ruling is untouched. The
         Direct3D 11 and Vulkan packages carry the same grant, added by their own ring rows. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="KhaozEngine.TestSupport.Gpu" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
